{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D5zCbvcJ5L2oosgeAwuoh8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Bootstrapping and Migrating Documentation\n2\t\n3\t## Detect before proposing\n4\t\n5\t“Adopt OKF for our engineering docs” calls for reviewing the populated\n6\tcollection as it stands at checkout. Valid frontmatter can coexist with two\n7\tanswers to the same question or a release command separated from its stop\n8\tcondition.\n9\t\n10\t| Request                                          | Work and completion claim                                                                                                  |\n11\t| ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n12\t| Start a new or empty bundle                      | Answer the [first reader question](#start-with-a-reader).                                                                  |\n13\t| Migrate a populated root or package              | [Assess the collection](#assess-the-collection), design its subject layout, then migrate and verify the covered subjects.  |\n14\t| Add frontmatter only                             | Make the requested format changes and report the format checks. Leave the editorial outcome open.                          |\n15\t| Fix one page or keep docs current after a change | [Update affected claims and callers](./consulting-and-upkeep.md#find-the-affected-claim). Bound the result to that change. |\n16\t\n17\t**Guidelines:**\n18\t\n19\t- MUST identify the requested root or package, its entry points and its export boundary before interpreting the [whole-root check](./validators.md#check-the-whole-documentation-root).\n20\t- MUST preserve a working legacy layout until the project adopts migration. Follow its change gates, then carry an approved migration through to the owning pages and callers.\n21\t\n22\t## Start with a reader\n23\t\n24\tOne recurring question is enough to begin. For a new bundle the root index can\n25\tbegin with this frontmatter, followed by a topic heading and a link to the\n26\tconcept.\n27\t\n28\t```yaml\n29\t---\n30\tokf_version: \"0.3\"\n31\ttitle: Scheduler engineering\n32\tdescription: Job execution and delivery guarantees for Scheduler contributors.\n33\t---\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\tIn a small library, the rest of that index can be a single link:\n37\t`[When will a failed job run again?](./retries.md)`. The whole bundle is\n38\t`index.md` and `retries.md`. The concept could read:\n39\t\n40\t```markdown\n41\t---\n42\ttype: Specification\n43\ttitle: Job retries\n44\tdescription: Retry timing, attempt limits and the state callers observe between attempts.\n45\t---\n46\t\n47\t# Job retries\n48\t\n49\tA failed job waits one minute for a second attempt and appears as queued.\n50\tIf the second attempt fails, the job ends as failed.\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\tHere the retry contract owns both timing and exhaustion. When readers also\n54\tneed to count attempts, a growing project can group a Definition and a\n55\tSpecification under scheduling. The [Scheduler subject index](../assets/docs-example/scheduling/index.md)\n56\tshows that choice in a larger service with its own retry limits. Link both\n57\tthe human contributor guide and agent instructions to the same maintained\n58\t[placement convention](../assets/docs-example/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).\n59\t\n60\t**Guidelines:**\n61\t\n62\t- SHOULD answer the first question from the implementation or an adopted decision, give the answer a primary type, and link it from the index.\n63\t- SHOULD let a new decision earn its own page. Link it from the flat root or from the subject where readers already seek it.\n64\t\n65\t## Assess the collection\n66\t\n67\tStart at the published root as someone who knows the product term but has\n68\tnever seen this repository. Then try a caller with a narrower question, such\n69\tas a release operator arriving from a checklist. A contributor placing the\n70\tnext related contract is another reader of the layout.\n71\t\n72\t**Guidelines:**\n73\t\n74\t- MUST inventory the initial tree across the requested scope. Include indexes, glossaries, human and agent entry points, external callers, tools and boundaries such as generated pages with a separate producer or immutable evidence.\n75\t- MUST [trace representative reader questions](./consulting-and-upkeep.md#verify-a-readers-route) in the existing collection before choosing a target layout.\n76\t- SHOULD scan every subject at page level in a large corpus, then inspect suspect sections and decision-critical routes closely. Look for mixed decisions, conflicting or repeated rules, unfamiliar terms, claims without support, questions without an owner and prerequisites stranded on other pages.\n77\t\n78\t## Design the target\n79\t\n80\tA root labeled “Specifications” and “Guides” asks a newcomer to predict\n81\twhich kind of document contains the answer. A reader who asks when a job\n82\tbecomes terminal can choose “Scheduling.” An operator deciding whether to\n83\troll back can choose “Operating a release.” Try both routes in the\n84\t[worked Scheduler migration](../assets/scheduler-migration.md).\n85\t\n86\t**Guidelines:**\n87\t\n88\t- SHOULD sketch the smallest tree that answers the observed questions. Name each subject in the project's language and say which decisions it owns. Choose [page types and local indexes](./documentation-structure.md#choose-the-subject) once the owners are clear.\n89\t- SHOULD compare a minimal repair, such as a direct heading link, with a different grouping when ownership is ambiguous. Try a root lookup, [placing a new claim from a contributor entry point](./conventions-and-operations.md#contributor-routes) and the affected caller routes. Weigh the cost of maintaining old entries.\n90\t- MUST account for each affected legacy decision in working notes or the existing change record. Identify what stays, what moves or splits, which claims merge at one owner, and why a page is created or retired.\n91\t- MUST investigate missing answers in implementation, tests, configuration or an adopted decision. Write each supported answer at its owner. Where evidence conflicts or runs out, record the exact question and which decision it leaves open.\n92\t\n93\t## Migrate one reading route\n94\t\n95\tA metric's formula, its result-buffer contract and its presentation can change\n96\tindependently. A sampled stage can compute values for several participants\n97\twhile returning a score for only one. A caller needs the result Specification\n98\tto know whose score is available. By contrast, a configuration name, its\n99\trequiredness and its failure behavior together tell the operator what to set.\n100\tA testing convention and the counterexample that motivated it also belong\n101\ttogether.\n102\t\n103\t**Guidelines:**\n104\t\n105\t- MUST trace the slice's claims to their owners, callers, paths and fragments, exported assets, metadata and supporting evidence before editing.\n106\t- SHOULD split claims when they serve different decisions and can change independently. Keep facts that a reader must apply together on one page; page length and heading count alone do not justify a split.\n107\t- MUST carry each claim's limits, rationale and evidence into the typed owner, preserving established type names and existing metadata. Update generated material at its producer. Reconcile verified duplicates and retire obsolete material within the agreed scope.\n108\t- MUST [preserve the reading routes](./cross-references.md#preserve-the-reading-route) affected by each slice. Update the subject index and glossary where readers use them.\n109\t- MUST preserve the action and any condition that changes it at the point of decision, including authority, cost, recovery or a safe no-op. Keep an ordered trace together when its sequence explains the outcome. Prefer a heading or link when splitting would make readers reconstruct one decision.\n110\t- MUST read each extracted page independently, exercise a boundary case and [trace the affected entry points](./consulting-and-upkeep.md#verify-a-readers-route).\n111\t\n112\t## Close the requested scope\n113\t\n114\tIn a flat library, a retry page can already answer both “When is the next\n115\tattempt?” and “What happens after the last one?” Opening that page from the\n116\troot and exercising exhaustion can justify keeping the layout. A local index\n117\twith only that destination would give the reader another step.\n118\t\n119\t**Guidelines:**\n120\t\n121\t- MUST reconcile the edited tree with the initial inventory. For staged work, record covered subjects, remaining owners, unresolved questions and cross-package callers in the existing change record. Limit the completion claim to the subjects examined.\n122\t- MUST report [format checks](./validators.md) separately from editorial results. Replay representative questions from the root and callers. For a repaired defect, show the original failure, the edited owner and the route to the complete answer. For a retained layout, identify a working owner, its boundary case and the detour an extra split or directory would introduce.\n123\t\n124\t## Preserve evidence identity\n125\t\n126\tAn approval that names a file by SHA-256 binds to its bytes, including its first\n127\tline and newline convention. When retiring a dated feasibility study, move any still-useful check into the\n128\toperator's Procedure. Its next execution produces evidence for that change's\n129\tpull request. A reader investigating the old result can open the original\n130\tstudy at its commit.\n131\t\n132\t**Guidelines:**\n133\t\n134\t- MUST preserve any bytes an existing approval or distribution contract binds. If current guidance relies on that evidence, cite its pinned source and acceptance from the typed page that owns the claim. An existing Specification or Procedure can be its companion.\n135\t- MUST distinguish implemented behavior, an adopted target awaiting implementation, a revision-specific observation and an unverified hypothesis in the wording of the claim. State which revision, environment and conditions the evidence supports. Preserve recorded provenance without inferring verification, authorship, ownership, approval or license from migration.\n136\t- SHOULD remove superseded reports from the live tree once active evidence obligations and reader routes are accounted for. Direct historical lookups to the original commit or pull request.\n137\t- MUST record hashes before migration and compare the exported evidence and linked assets byte-for-byte with their originals.\n138\t- MUST report intentional untyped evidence by path during verification so an accidental omission elsewhere remains visible.\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6b8195-6831-4a69-8441-b74ebe8863d9","uuid":"920dbe23-df02-4f0d-ba90-27b7a6d78640","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:11:48.209Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/skill-evaluation-0NIi1k/.claude/skills/living-project-documentation/references/bootstrapping.md","content":"# Bootstrapping and Migrating Documentation\n\n## Detect before proposing\n\n“Adopt OKF for our engineering docs” calls for reviewing the populated\ncollection as it stands at checkout. Valid frontmatter can coexist with two\nanswers to the same question or a release command separated from its stop\ncondition.\n\n| Request                                          | Work and completion claim                                                                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Start a new or empty bundle                      | Answer the [first reader question](#start-with-a-reader).                       