{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NQVENTAeCWJ3QAk5qjjmFE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Deploy hooks\n2\t\n3\tOperational notes on what happens after a post is published — the part that\n4\tdoesn't belong in a code comment because it spans two files (`server/app.ts`\n5\tand `server/deploy-hook.ts`) and one decision worth explaining once rather\n6\tthan re-deriving at either call site.\n7\t\n8\t## What publishing actually does\n9\t\n10\tPublishing a post is three steps, in order:\n11\t\n12\t1. Write a `revisions` row — a snapshot of the post's title and body at the\n13\t   moment of publish.\n14\t2. Flip the post's `status` to `published`.\n15\t3. Call the site's `deployHookUrl`.\n16\t\n17\tThe first two are a single logical write and either both happen or neither\n18\tdoes. The third is best-effort: a site's deploy hook is usually a webhook on\n19\tsomeone else's static host (Vercel, Netlify, and similar all expose one), and\n20\tthose are occasionally slow or briefly unavailable — worth a retry, not worth\n21\tlosing the author's published content over.\n22\t\n23\t## Why a failed deploy hook doesn't fail the publish\n24\t\n25\tBy the time `triggerDeployHook` runs, the post and its revision are already\n26\tcommitted. Rolling that back because a webhook timed out would throw away\n27\treal work over a problem that's usually transient and, worse, outside this\n28\tapp's control entirely. Instead, the publish response carries\n29\t`deployTriggered: boolean`, and the editor shows the author which case they're\n30\tin — see `PostEditorPage.tsx`'s toast copy.\n31\t\n32\t## The retry policy\n33\t\n34\tThree attempts, with a linear backoff between them. That's tuned for \"a cold\n35\tserverless function taking a moment to wake up,\" not for a target that's\n36\tgenuinely down — three attempts a few hundred milliseconds apart won't save a\n37\tpublish from a deploy hook that's been deleted or misconfigured. When every\n38\tattempt fails, `deploy-hook.ts` logs the exhaustion at `error` (there's no\n39\terror tracker on the API side to hand it to instead — see `shared/logger.ts`)\n40\tand returns `{ ok: false }` rather than throwing, so the route above always\n41\tgets a definite answer either way.\n42\t\n43\t## Rolling back a bad publish\n44\t\n45\tThere's no unpublish button in this cut of the product. The revision a\n46\tpublish writes is a record of what went out, not an undo mechanism —\n47\treverting live content means editing the post back to the previous\n48\trevision's text and publishing again.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6b8195-6831-4a69-8441-b74ebe8863d9","uuid":"7cc93d5c-46e0-4c44-a00f-544ce64204dc","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:11:56.823Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/skill-evaluation-0NIi1k/docs/deploy-hooks.md","content":"# Deploy hooks\n\nOperational notes on what happens after a post is published — the part that\ndoesn't belong in a code comment because it spans two files (`server/app.ts`\nand `server/deploy-hook.ts`) and one decision worth explaining once rather\nthan re-deriving at either call site.\n\n## What publishing actually does\n\nPublishing a post is three steps, in order:\n\n1. Write a `revisions` row — a snapshot of the post's title and body at the\n   moment of publish.\n2. Flip the post's `status` to `published`.\n3. Call the site's `deployHookUrl`.\n\nThe first two are a single logical write and either both happen or neither\ndoes. The third is best-effort: a site's deploy hook is usually a webhook on\nsomeone else's static host (Vercel, Netlify, and similar all expose one), and\nthose are occasionally slow or briefly unavailable — worth a retry, not worth\nlosing the author's published content over.\n\n## Why a failed deploy hook doesn't fail the publish\n\nBy the time `triggerDeployHook` runs, the post and its revision are already\ncommitted. Rolling that back because a webhook timed out would throw away\nreal work over a problem that's usually transient and, worse, outside this\napp's control entirely. Instead, the publish response carries\n`deployTriggered: boolean`, and the editor shows the author which case they're\nin — see `PostEditorPage.tsx`'s toast copy.\n\n## The retry policy\n\nThree attempts, with a linear backoff between them. That's tuned for \"a cold\nserverless function taking a moment to wake up,\" not for a target that's\ngenuinely down — three attempts a few hundred milliseconds apart won't save a\npublish from a deploy hook that's been deleted or misconfigured. When every\nattempt fails, `deploy-hook.ts` logs the exhaustion at `error` (there's no\nerror tracker on the API side to hand it to instead — see `shared/logger.ts`)\nand returns `{ ok: false }` rather than throwing, so the route above always\ngets a definite answer either way.\n\n## Rolling back a bad publish\n\nThere's no unpublish button in this cut of the product. The revision a\npublish writes is a record of what went out, not an undo mechanism —\nreverting live content means editing the post back to the previous\nrevision's text and publishing again.\n","numLines":49,"startLine":1,"totalLines":49}}}
