{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzzuWd8XhBEtaJKE1YFYfd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Conventions and Operations\n2\t\n3\t## Placing a project rule\n4\t\n5\tWhere a violation appears helps identify the reader who needs the rule. A\n6\tclient receiving a missing field encounters a contract violation. A reviewer\n7\tchecking directory layout is applying a Convention, even if a linter enforces\n8\tit. An operator diagnosing a queue backlog needs the Architecture to identify\n9\tthe producer and consumer before consulting a recovery Procedure.\n10\t\n11\t**Guidelines:**\n12\t\n13\t- SHOULD place an observable guarantee in its Specification and a team's choice among conforming practices in its Convention.\n14\t- MUST keep the constraint beside its reasoning and link to its existing owner when already documented. A project with architectural decision records MAY link the original record for provenance.\n15\t\n16\t## Permission and execution\n17\t\n18\tA release operator activates a rollout. A device operator checks the\n19\tinstallation and restores service if verification fails. Both procedures can\n20\tdepend on the same permission to release. When one operator carries out a\n21\tsingle task, a locally scoped permission check can fit directly in its steps.\n22\t\n23\t**Guidelines:**\n24\t\n25\t- SHOULD extract a Policy when several entry points depend on a permission rule that changes independently.\n26\t- MUST identify the adopted scope, exception authority and reopening conditions, and connect every affected entry point, including manual and emergency paths, to the actual authority.\n27\t- SHOULD give operators separate procedures when their tasks have different prerequisites, success signals or recovery actions.\n28\t- MUST state the local stop condition beside a consequential step. A successful recovery command establishes what ran. Permission to run it comes from the project's authority.\n29\t- MUST NOT invent an approver or independent permission rule to populate a type.\n30\t\n31\t## Contributor routes\n32\t\n33\tA contributor guide can direct a new maintainer to the setup procedure and a\n34\treviewer to the same convention used by the linter. Each route leads to one\n35\tmaintained rule.\n36\t\n37\t**Guidelines:**\n38\t\n39\t- SHOULD document each subject's scope and its nearest confusing neighbor. Give a tie-breaker a contributor can apply to a new claim and explain when to add a subject. For instance, a worker-pool cap belongs with dispatch internals unless it promises an outcome to callers.\n40\t- SHOULD link the placement convention from durable human and agent entry points and update it when responsibility moves. Link editable callers directly to their governing section.\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6b8195-6831-4a69-8441-b74ebe8863d9","uuid":"37fad5c2-2f1a-44b2-adc7-c5ea55bf7d28","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:12:21.256Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/skill-evaluation-0NIi1k/.claude/skills/living-project-documentation/references/conventions-and-operations.md","content":"# Conventions and Operations\n\n## Placing a project rule\n\nWhere a violation appears helps identify the reader who needs the rule. A\nclient receiving a missing field encounters a contract violation. A reviewer\nchecking directory layout is applying a Convention, even if a linter enforces\nit. An operator diagnosing a queue backlog needs the Architecture to identify\nthe producer and consumer before consulting a recovery Procedure.\n\n**Guidelines:**\n\n- SHOULD place an observable guarantee in its Specification and a team's choice among conforming practices in its Convention.\n- MUST keep the constraint beside its reasoning and link to its existing owner when already documented. A project with architectural decision records MAY link the original record for provenance.\n\n## Permission and execution\n\nA release operator activates a rollout. A device operator checks the\ninstallation and restores service if verification fails. Both procedures can\ndepend on the same permission to release. When one operator carries out a\nsingle task, a locally scoped permission check can fit directly in its steps.\n\n**Guidelines:**\n\n- SHOULD extract a Policy when several entry points depend on a permission rule that changes independently.\n- MUST identify the adopted scope, exception authority and reopening conditions, and connect every affected entry point, including manual and emergency paths, to the actual authority.\n- SHOULD give operators separate procedures when their tasks have different prerequisites, success signals or recovery actions.\n- MUST state the local stop condition beside a consequential step. A successful recovery command establishes what ran. Permission to run it comes from the project's authority.\n- MUST NOT invent an approver or independent permission rule to populate a type.\n\n## Contributor routes\n\nA contributor guide can direct a new maintainer to the setup procedure and a\nreviewer to the same convention used by the linter. Each route leads to one\nmaintained rule.\n\n**Guidelines:**\n\n- SHOULD document each subject's scope and its nearest confusing neighbor. Give a tie-breaker a contributor can apply to a new claim and explain when to add a subject. For instance, a worker-pool cap belongs with dispatch internals unless it promises an outcome to callers.\n- SHOULD link the placement convention from durable human and agent entry points and update it when responsibility moves. Link editable callers directly to their governing section.\n","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41}}}
