{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HFs7FhGUu3TYzgBEGsVw4M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Migrating the Scheduler handbook\n2\t\n3\t“Please migrate the Scheduler engineering docs to OKF.” The example follows\n4\tthe job outcomes, notification rules and release procedure through that change.\n5\tThe trees show the paths involved. The [finished bundle](./docs-example/index.md)\n6\talso covers job creation, worker dispatch and contribution practices.\n7\t\n8\t## Before\n9\t\n10\t```text\n11\tdocs/\n12\t├── index.md\n13\t├── specs/\n14\t│   └── jobs.md\n15\t└── guides/\n16\t    └── handbook.md\n17\t```\n18\t\n19\tThe root offered **Specifications → Jobs** and **Guides → Handbook**.\n20\tStarting there, a new API client author asking “Can a cancelled job run\n21\tagain if it still has retries?” had to guess the document type.\n22\t`specs/jobs.md#states` listed `cancelled` among the possible states.\n23\tIts retry section said “A failed job may be retried three times.” Neither\n24\tsection answered the cancellation question.\n25\t\n26\tThe handbook mixed three decisions.\n27\t\n28\t- **Notifications.** “A rule fires on a terminal state. Check the job states\n29\t  to decide which outcomes qualify.” Its link pointed to `../specs/jobs.md#states`.\n30\t- **Checking a release.** “Watch the claim rate for five minutes. Jobs\n31\t  waiting for a retry appear as queued. A job gets three tries in all.”\n32\t- **Undoing a release.** “Use the recorded previous revision. If a destructive\n33\t  contract migration already removed data, stop and hand the restore to\n34\t  on-call. Otherwise run `npm run deploy -- --revision <previous>`.”\n35\t\n36\t## Target and evidence\n37\t\n38\tAdding links to the handbook headings would shorten the operator's route.\n39\tIt would still leave the API contract divided between the Jobs specification\n40\tand a release check. Changing the retry budget would require finding both\n41\taccounts. A subject entry for scheduling can give client authors the job\n42\tcontract, while the operator can change the release check independently.\n43\t\n44\tIn this illustrative checkout the adopted decision `decisions/job-lifecycle.md`\n45\tsays, “A failed attempt returns to the queue while a retry remains. Allow three retries after\n46\tthe initial try. Cancellation ends the job regardless of the retry budget.”\n47\tThe worker's transition table dispatches retries only on failure. The\n48\tcancellation test in `worker/cancel.test.ts` exercises the limiting case.\n49\t\n50\t```typescript\n51\tconst job = await worker.startJob({ retriesRemaining: 2 });\n52\tawait worker.cancel(job.id);\n53\texpect(await queue.pendingAttempts(job.id)).toHaveLength(0);\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t| Legacy passage or question                        | Destination and reason                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n57\t| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n58\t| Jobs / States and the missing cancellation answer | Move the state contract to Scheduling / Job outcomes. Add what happens on cancellation with retries left from the adopted decision.                                                                    |\n59\t| Jobs / Retries and Handbook / Checking a release  | Consolidate the conflicting counts at Job outcomes. Extract the public `queued` behavior from the runbook. Keep the five-minute claim-rate check in Deployment and link directly to the retry passage. |\n60\t| Handbook / Notifications                          | Move the rules to Notifications. Keep the decision to send a message here and point its state lookup directly to Job outcomes.                                                                         |\n61\t| Handbook / Undoing a release                      | Keep the rollback command, its authority and the stop/restore branch together in Deployment. An operator about to redeploy needs all three.                                                            |\n62\t\n63\tOne client question remains open. How long after cancellation can an external\n64\tprocess keep writing? The transition test observes the job state and retry\n65\tqueue. It measures no process shutdown time, and the adopted decision gives\n66\tno deadline. Record that clients cannot choose an output-read timeout from\n67\tthis evidence. Leave the deadline out of the contract.\n68\t\n69\t## After\n70\t\n71\t```text\n72\tdocs/\n73\t├── index.md\n74\t├── scheduling/\n75\t│   ├── index.md\n76\t│   ├── jobs.md\n77\t│   ├── outcomes.md\n78\t│   ├── attempts.md\n79\t│   └── dispatch.md\n80\t├── notifications.md\n81\t└── operating/\n82\t    └── deployment.md\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\tReplay the client's question from the [revised root](./docs-example/index.md).\n86\t**Scheduling → Which job states are terminal?** lands on “A cancelled job\n87\treceives no automatic retry, even if retries remain.” Someone starting at the\n88\tglossary with the dashboard term “Retries remaining” reaches the retry contract.\n89\t[Scheduling index](./docs-example/scheduling/index.md) · [Owning state contract](./docs-example/scheduling/outcomes.md#states) · [Dashboard term](./docs-example/glossary.md)\n90\t\n91\tStart again at the edited caller. [Notification rules](./docs-example/notifications.md#rules)\n92\tlinks “which states are terminal, including cancellation with retries left”\n93\tdirectly to the contract. [Verifying a release](./docs-example/operating/deployment.md#verifying-a-release)\n94\tnow links to the retry passage and tells the operator to check the claim rate\n95\tbefore diagnosing a stall. At [Rolling back](./docs-example/operating/deployment.md#rolling-back),\n96\tsomeone who has already removed data encounters the stop and handoff before\n97\tthe command.\n98\t\n99\tTry placing a neighboring contract about the state a client sees while a\n100\tjob waits for its first worker. Scheduling owns the state returned to the\n101\tclient. Notifications owns whether a recipient is told about it. Two entry\n102\tpoints route contributors to that [placement rule](./docs-example/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).\n103\t\n104\t| Project entry | Edited instruction                                                                                                     |\n105\t| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n106\t| `README.md`   | `When contributing docs, [choose the owning subject](./docs/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).`          |\n107\t| `AGENTS.md`   | `Before adding a contract, consult the [placement convention](./docs/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).` |\n108\t\n109\tIn this example the published bookmark `index.md#specifications` is\n110\tsupported. The [retained heading](./docs-example/index.md#specifications)\n111\tlinks to the subject owners. The project approved retiring the two internal\n112\tlegacy page paths after switching their editable callers. Record those path\n113\tdecisions alongside the change. The work record marks job outcomes,\n114\tnotifications and release operations covered, with the shutdown-time\n115\tquestion unresolved. Job creation and dispatch still need their own reader\n116\tchecks before the whole collection can be reported as migrated.\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6b8195-6831-4a69-8441-b74ebe8863d9","uuid":"ba19a6a8-5fe3-458d-b800-c8407c66302d","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:14:31.632Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/skill-evaluation-0NIi1k/.claude/skills/living-project-documentation/assets/scheduler-migration.md","content":"# Migrating the Scheduler handbook\n\n“Please migrate the Scheduler engineering docs to OKF.” The example follows\nthe job outcomes, notification rules and release procedure through that change.\nThe trees show the paths involved. The [finished bundle](./docs-example/index.md)\nalso covers job creation, worker dispatch and contribution practices.\n\n## Before\n\n```text\ndocs/\n├── index.md\n├── specs/\n│   └── jobs.md\n└── guides/\n    └── handbook.md\n```\n\nThe root offered **Specifications → Jobs** and **Guides → Handbook**.\nStarting there, a new API client author asking “Can a cancelled job run\nagain if it still has retries?” had to guess the document type.\n`specs/jobs.md#states` listed `cancelled` among the possible states.\nIts retry section said “A failed job may be retried three times.” Neither\nsection answered the cancellation question.\n\nThe handbook mixed three decisions.\n\n- **Notifications.** “A rule fires on a terminal state. Check the job states\n  to decide which outcomes qualify.” Its link pointed to `../specs/jobs.md#states`.\n- **Checking a release.** “Watch the claim rate for five minutes. Jobs\n  waiting for a retry appear as queued. A job gets three tries in all.”\n- **Undoing a release.** “Use the recorded previous revision. If a destructive\n  contract migration already removed data, stop and hand the restore to\n  on-call. Otherwise run `npm run deploy -- --revision <previous>`.”\n\n## Target and evidence\n\nAdding links to the handbook headings would shorten the operator's route.\nIt would still leave the API contract divided between the Jobs specification\nand a release check. Changing the retry budget would require finding both\naccounts. A subject entry for scheduling can give client authors the job\ncontract, while the operator can change the release check independently.\n\nIn this illustrative checkout the adopted decision `decisions/job-lifecycle.md`\nsays, “A failed attempt returns to the queue while a retry remains. Allow three retries after\nthe initial try. Cancellation ends the job regardless of the retry budget.”\nThe worker's transition table dispatches retries only on failure. The\ncancellation test in `worker/cancel.test.ts` exercises the limiting case.\n\n```typescript\nconst job = await worker.startJob({ retriesRemaining: 2 });\nawait worker.cancel(job.id);\nexpect(await queue.pendingAttempts(job.id)).toHaveLength(0);\n```\n\n| Legacy passage or question                        | Destination and reason                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Jobs / States and the missing cancellation answer | Move the state contract to Scheduling / Job outcomes. Add what happens on cancellation with retries left from the adopted decision.                                                                    |\n| Jobs / Retries and Handbook / Checking a release  | Consolidate the conflicting counts at Job outcomes. Extract the public `queued` behavior from the runbook. Keep the five-minute claim-rate check in Deployment and link directly to the retry passage. |\n| Handbook / Notifications                          | Move the rules to Notifications. Keep the decision to send a message here and point its state lookup directly to Job outcomes.                                                                         |\n| Handbook / Undoing a release                      | Keep the rollback command, its authority and the stop/restore branch together in Deployment. An operator about to redeploy needs all three.                                                            |\n\nOne client question remains open. How long after cancellation can an external\nprocess keep writing? The transition test observes the job state and retry\nqueue. It measures no process shutdown time, and the adopted decision gives\nno deadline. Record that clients cannot choose an output-read timeout from\nthis evidence. Leave the deadline out of the contract.\n\n## After\n\n```text\ndocs/\n├── index.md\n├── scheduling/\n│   ├── index.md\n│   ├── jobs.md\n│   ├── outcomes.md\n│   ├── attempts.md\n│   └── dispatch.md\n├── notifications.md\n└── operating/\n    └── deployment.md\n```\n\nReplay the client's question from the [revised root](./docs-example/index.md).\n**Scheduling → Which job states are terminal?** lands on “A cancelled job\nreceives no automatic retry, even if retries remain.” Someone starting at the\nglossary with the dashboard term “Retries remaining” reaches the retry contract.\n[Scheduling index](./docs-example/scheduling/index.md) · [Owning state contract](./docs-example/scheduling/outcomes.md#states) · [Dashboard term](./docs-example/glossary.md)\n\nStart again at the edited caller. [Notification rules](./docs-example/notifications.md#rules)\nlinks “which states are terminal, including cancellation with retries left”\ndirectly to the contract. [Verifying a release](./docs-example/operating/deployment.md#verifying-a-release)\nnow links to the retry passage and tells the operator to check the claim rate\nbefore diagnosing a stall. At [Rolling back](./docs-example/operating/deployment.md#rolling-back),\nsomeone who has already removed data encounters the stop and handoff before\nthe command.\n\nTry placing a neighboring contract about the state a client sees while a\njob waits for its first worker. Scheduling owns the state returned to the\nclient. Notifications owns whether a recipient is told about it. Two entry\npoints route contributors to that [placement rule](./docs-example/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).\n\n| Project entry | Edited instruction                                                                                                     |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `README.md`   | `When contributing docs, [choose the owning subject](./docs/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).`          |\n| `AGENTS.md`   | `Before adding a contract, consult the [placement convention](./docs/contributing/documentation.md#choose-a-subject).` |\n\nIn this example the published bookmark `index.md#specifications` is\nsupported. The [retained heading](./docs-example/index.md#specifications)\nlinks to the subject owners. The project approved retiring the two internal\nlegacy page paths after switching their editable callers. Record those path\ndecisions alongside the change. The work record marks job outcomes,\nnotifications and release operations covered, with the shutdown-time\nquestion unresolved. Job creation and dispatch still need their own reader\nchecks before the whole collection can be reported as migrated.\n","numLines":117,"startLine":1,"totalLines":117}}}
