{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01645MdaJn5KQfu6yxFsGGut","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: Procedure\n3\ttitle: Deployment\n4\tdescription: How the release operator migrates the schema, deploys, verifies and rolls back Scheduler.\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# Deployment\n8\t\n9\tThe release operator uses a checkout of the release revision with production\n10\tdeployment access. Only the release operator may deploy or roll back, even when\n11\tsomeone else has credentials that can run the command. Before release, record\n12\tthe previous revision for rollback.\n13\t\n14\t## Releasing\n15\t\n16\t```bash\n17\tnpm run build\n18\tnpm run migrate:up -- --env production\n19\tnpm run deploy\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tMigrations MUST run before the deploy, never after. The new code assumes the new\n23\tschema and the old code tolerates it, because every migration here is written to be\n24\tsafe against the version already running. Deploying first inverts that and opens a\n25\twindow where live code queries columns that do not exist yet.\n26\t\n27\tThe worker package deploys from the same command and the same commit. It MUST NOT be\n28\tdeployed on its own to pick up a fix: the two packages share `shared/`, and a split\n29\tdeploy runs two versions of it against one queue.\n30\t\n31\t## Verifying a release\n32\t\n33\tThe deploy is not finished when the command returns. Watch the worker's claim rate for\n34\tfive minutes and confirm queued jobs are being claimed. A release that broke the\n35\tqueue consumer can deploy cleanly and stop claiming while requests still succeed.\n36\t\n37\tQueued jobs include those [waiting for a retry](../scheduling/outcomes.md#attempts-and-retries).\n38\tCheck the claim rate before treating a queued job as evidence of a stalled worker.\n39\t\n40\t## Rolling back\n41\t\n42\tAs release operator, first check whether a destructive contract migration\n43\talready removed data. If it\n44\tdid, stop and hand the restore to the on-call operator. Deploying an older\n45\trevision cannot bring the data back.\n46\t\n47\tFor a code rollback, use the recorded previous revision that works with the\n48\tcurrent schema:\n49\t\n50\t```bash\n51\tnpm run deploy -- --revision <previous>\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tLeave the migration in place. Destructive changes have separate expand and\n55\tcontract migrations so the earlier code can run against the expanded schema.\n56\tRoll back to the revision before the contract and correct the schema with a\n57\tforward migration.\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c6b8195-6831-4a69-8441-b74ebe8863d9","uuid":"aebe18e7-567a-40bc-aec0-d727b1fac188","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:14:33.180Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/skill-evaluation-0NIi1k/.claude/skills/living-project-documentation/assets/docs-example/operating/deployment.md","content":"---\ntype: Procedure\ntitle: Deployment\ndescription: How the release operator migrates the schema, deploys, verifies and rolls back Scheduler.\n---\n\n# Deployment\n\nThe release operator uses a checkout of the release revision with production\ndeployment access. Only the release operator may deploy or roll back, even when\nsomeone else has credentials that can run the command. Before release, record\nthe previous revision for rollback.\n\n## Releasing\n\n```bash\nnpm run build\nnpm run migrate:up -- --env production\nnpm run deploy\n```\n\nMigrations MUST run before the deploy, never after. The new code assumes the new\nschema and the old code tolerates it, because every migration here is written to be\nsafe against the version already running. Deploying first inverts that and opens a\nwindow where live code queries columns that do not exist yet.\n\nThe worker package deploys from the same command and the same commit. It MUST NOT be\ndeployed on its own to pick up a fix: the two packages share `shared/`, and a split\ndeploy runs two versions of it against one queue.\n\n## Verifying a release\n\nThe deploy is not finished when the command returns. Watch the worker's claim rate for\nfive minutes and confirm queued jobs are being claimed. A release that broke the\nqueue consumer can deploy cleanly and stop claiming while requests still succeed.\n\nQueued jobs include those [waiting for a retry](../scheduling/outcomes.md#attempts-and-retries).\nCheck the claim rate before treating a queued job as evidence of a stalled worker.\n\n## Rolling back\n\nAs release operator, first check whether a destructive contract migration\nalready removed data. If it\ndid, stop and hand the restore to the on-call operator. Deploying an older\nrevision cannot bring the data back.\n\nFor a code rollback, use the recorded previous revision that works with the\ncurrent schema:\n\n```bash\nnpm run deploy -- --revision <previous>\n```\n\nLeave the migration in place. Destructive changes have separate expand and\ncontract migrations so the earlier code can run against the expanded schema.\nRoll back to the revision before the contract and correct the schema with a\nforward migration.\n","numLines":58,"startLine":1,"totalLines":58}}}
